\section{Higher direct images}\label{sec:direct}

Let $\mathcal G=\pi^{-1}F=G_F$ (Corollary~\ref{cor:sheaf}).

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:vanish}
For every $b\in B_P$ and $q>0$: $H^q(i(b),\mathcal G)=0$, and
$H^0(i(b),\mathcal G)=F(b)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Sheaf cohomology at the object $X=i(b)$ of the site $A_P^{\op}$ is the
colimit over hypercovers of Čech cohomology (Verdier's hypercovering
theorem). We exhibit a cover whose full Čech nerve is already cofinal for
$\mathcal G$-computations and has the stated cohomology. Take the
\emph{atlas} cover
\[
\mathcal U_0:=\{i(b)\to S_v: v\text{ a tight point of } i(b)\},
\]
where the family runs over all tight points (a set: subcones of
$P^{\gp}$). It is a cover: given a valuative $u:P\to V'$, factor $u$
through the valuative $V:=(P^{\gp}/(\ker u)^{\gp},\Sigma(u))$; the map
$P\to V$ is tight for $i(b)$ upto-the-face... for the atlas statement we
may by Corollary~\ref{cor:Bval} restrict to $B$-valued refinements. The
Čech nerve has
$\mathcal U_n=\prod\bigl(\mathcal G(S_{v_0}\otimes\cdots\otimes
S_{v_n})\bigr)$. Because every $S_v$ is tight, $B(S_v)=b$; crucially the
overlap kernels also stay at $b$: by Theorem~\ref{thm:nojump} applied with
$U:=S_{v_1}$ (whose lineality $L_{S_{v_1}}=J^{\gp}$,
$J=\Phi_{i(b)}$), the cone $\Sigma_{v_0}\supseteq Z_{S_{v_1}}$ is
\emph{not} automatic—so overlaps of atlas pieces can jump. We therefore
\emph{shrink the atlas}: keep only the subfamily $\mathcal V_b$ of tight
points whose cones contain the global cone... that cannot be required. Use
instead the following comparability-free device: the identity object
$i(b)$ is itself the tight piece of any tight cone... no, tight pieces are
CFA quotients of cover pieces, not covers of the identity.

Cleanest (standard): use descent for the sheaf $\mathcal G$
(Corollary~\ref{cor:sheaf}) which is \emph{already known}: for the sheaf
$\mathcal G$, the Čech-to-derived map degenerates: we compute sheaf
cohomology $H^q(i(b),\mathcal G)$ via the flasque Godement resolution
restricted... no. Standard fact: for any sheaf $\mathcal G$ and any cover
$\mathcal U$ of $X$, there is the local-to-global spectral sequence
$E_2^{pq}=\check H^p(\mathcal U,\underline{H}^q(\mathcal G))
\Rightarrow H^{p+q}(X,\mathcal G)$ where $\underline H^q$ is the presheaf
$U\mapsto H^q(U,\mathcal G)$. If an object $U$ has
$H^q(U,\mathcal G)=0$ ($q>0$) for every $U$ in a cofinal family of
hypercovers, then $H^q(X,\mathcal G)=0$. We prove by induction on $q$ and
simultaneously for all $B$-objects: \emph{$H^q(i(b),\mathcal G)=0$ for all
$q>0$, all $b$.} Induction on $q$. For $q=1$: a class in
$H^1(i(b),\mathcal G)$ is represented on some hypercover; refine its
degree-0 term to a cover of atlas type $\{i(b)\to S_v\}$ containing the
$\Sigma$-adapted pieces for all overlaps (Corollary~\ref{cor:Bval}):
precisely: choose the tight cones $\Sigma_v$ successively so that in the
refined atlas every pair $(S_0,S_1)$ satisfies
$\Sigma_0\supseteq Z_{S_1}$: impossible globally, so use per-pair: the
Čech nerve pull-back over the pair has $B$-object $b$ when the cones are
adapted; refine \emph{the 1-cocycle's cover} pair by pair: the cocycle
condition lives on triples: adapt cones to triples: finite list: possible.
Then the augmented complex with the identity piece $S_{\mathrm{id}}:=$
(tight piece for a cone equal to a total order $\Sigma_{\mathrm{id}}$
adapted to all the finitely many cones in the list: a total cone
containing all $Z_{S_i}$: lineality of joint $Z$: $J^{\gp}$: possible
